  {
    "title": "Five Little Pigs",
    "type": "Novel",
    "character": "Hercule Poirot",
    "year": 1942,
    "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/five-little-pigs",
    "cover": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/hcus-paperback/FiveLittlePigs_PB.jpg",
    "synopsis": "Beautiful Caroline Crale was convicted of poisoning her husband, yet there were five other suspects: Philip Blake (the stockbroker) who went to market; Meredith Blake (the amateur herbalist) who stayed at home; Elsa Greer (the three-time divorcee) who had roast beef; Cecilia Williams (the devoted governess) who had none; and Angela Warren (the disfigured sister) who cried ‘wee wee wee’ all the way home. It is sixteen years later, but Hercule Poirot just can’t get that nursery rhyme out of his mind.",
    "moreAbout": "First published as a novel in the US in 1942, under the title Murder in Retrospect, the story shows Christie’s fascination with memory and time. Poirot is asked by a young woman to solve the mystery of her father’s murder, to clear her deceased mother’s name. Through the testimonies of those who were present on the day of the murder, Poirot must reconstruct the scene. Considered one of Christie’s hidden gems, this story shows Poirot at his best, studying the words of witnesses and reading between the lines.\n\nSimilarly to some of Christie's other works, Five Little Pigs gets its named name from a nursery rhyme, and each of the five main characters is perfectly reflected in the rhyme.\n\nChristie’s own adaptation of the novel into a play in 1960, saw her not only change the title to Go Back For Murder but also removed the character of Poirot entirely. In the play, Poirot's function is replaced by a young lawyer, Justin Fogg, son of the lawyer who led Caroline Crale's case.\n\nDavid Suchet starred in the 2003 TV adaptation, which included several major changes. The character of Philip Blake, rather than being in love with Caroline, was in fact in love with her husband Amyas, Caroline had been executed, and Carla’s name is changed to Lucy. In 2006, BBC Radio 4 adapted the story with favourite John Moffatt as Poirot. A graphic novel of the story was released in 2010.",
    "trivia": [
      "The crime scene is based closely on Greenway Estate with the geography of the story corresponding exactly with Greenway's grounds. As the movements of the victim are integral to the plot, parts of the estate are described in detail.",
      "The unusual name Amyas came from the cousin of friends who the young Agatha once pic-nicked with at Anstey’s Cove in Torquay."
    ],
    "quote": {
      "text": "One does not employ merely the muscles. I do not need to bend and measure the footprints and pick up the cigarette ends and examine the bent blades of grass. It is enough for me to sit back in my chair and think.",
      "author": "Hercule Poirot, Five Little Pigs"
    },
    "extractPdf": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/image-store/Read-Christie-2024/ACL_PoirotList_2024.pdf",
    "related": [
      {
        "title": "Dead Man's Folly",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/dead-mans-folly"
      },
      {
        "title": "Hercule Poirot's Christmas",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/hercule-poirots-christmas"
      },
      {
        "title": "Cards on the Table",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/cards-on-the-table"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "The Body in the Library",
    "type": "Novel",
    "character": "Miss Marple",
    "year": 1942,
    "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-body-in-the-library",
    "cover": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/hcus-paperback/Jacket_TheBodyInTheLibrary_US.jpg",
    "synopsis": "It’s seven in the morning. The Bantrys wake to find the body of a young woman in their library. She is wearing evening dress and heavy make-up, which is now smeared across her cheeks. But who is she? How did she get there? And what is the connection with another dead girl, whose charred remains are later discovered in an abandoned quarry? The respectable Bantrys invite Miss Marple to solve the mystery… before tongues start to wag.",
    "moreAbout": "One of Miss Marple’s finest cases, here we see her at the height of her female intuition, an inconspicuous elderly lady who can investigate undetected. Several other detectives get involved in the case - almost as many as there are suspects. Of course, it is Miss Marple who will unveil the ultimate clue.The novel was first released in February 1942 in the US and later that year in the UK. There is a rare example of Agatha Christie name checking herself, via the voice of Peter Carmody, who claims to love detective fiction and has signed copies by Christie and Dorothy L. Sayers among others. The novel’s title was an in-joke between Agatha Christie and her character Ariadne Oliver, who reveals in Christie’s earlier work Cards on the Table, that she herself had written a crime novel titled The Body in the Library.\n\nIt was adapted for TV in 1984, starring Joan Hickson as Marple, her first appearance in what would become an acclaimed role for her. It was broadcast in three parts over the Christmas period of that year. 2004 saw a more radical adaptation, with Geraldine McEwan as Miss Marple. It featured several well-known British actors, including Ian Richardson and Joanna Lumely. BBC Radio 4 dramatised the story in 2005, with June Whitfield reassuming her role as the radio Marple.",
    "trivia": [],
    "quote": {
      "text": "What I feel is that if one has got to have a murder actually happening in one’s house, one might as well enjoy it, if you know what I mean.",
      "author": "Mrs Bantry, The Body in the Library"
    },
    "extractPdf": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/news/Chapter1_The-Body-in-the-Library_%C2%A9AgathaChristieLimited.pdf",
    "related": [
      {
        "title": "A Caribbean Mystery",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/a-caribbean-mystery"
      },
      {
        "title": "The Murder at the Vicarage",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-murder-at-the-vicarage"
      },
      {
        "title": "The Mirror Crack'd from Side to Side",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-mirror-crackd-from-side-to-side"
      }
    ]
