  {
    "title": "The Mirror Crack'd from Side to Side",
    "type": "Novel",
    "character": "Miss Marple",
    "year": 1962,
    "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-mirror-crackd-from-side-to-side",
    "cover": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/hcus-paperback/Jacket_TheMirrorCrackdFromSideToSide_US.jpg",
    "synopsis": "One minute, silly Heather Badcock had been gabbling on at her movie idol, the glamorous Marina Gregg. The next, Heather suffered a massive seizure. But for whom was the deadly poison really intended?\n\nMarina’s frozen expression suggested she had witnessed something horrific. But, while others searched for material evidence, Jane Marple conducted a very different investigation – into human nature.",
    "moreAbout": "The last of Agatha Christie classic English village mysteries, St Mary Mead is no longer what it once was. The 1960s see a widowed Dolly Bantry (of The Body in the Library) forced to sell Gossington Hall, which is eventually purchased by a rich movie star who brings a deadly mix of glamour and torment into the now developing community. Observing the social changes following the Second World War, Agatha Christie and Miss Marple do what they've always done - solve the mystery through the habits of the people involve and the village they live in.\n\nAgatha Christie had long been fascinated by the tastes of new 'developed' houses on the estates around her home in Wallingford, Oxfordshire and she saw an opportunity to explore these in The Mirror Crack'd. The title came from the poem The Lady of Shalott, by Tennyson.\n\nIn 1980 Hollywood adapted the novel into a film starring Angela Lansbury as Miss Marple alongside Elizabeth Taylor as the beautiful Marina Gregg. They shortened the title to The Mirror Crack’d. In 1992 The Mirror Crack'd from Side to Side was the last Miss Marple novel to be adapted by the BBC and star Joan Hickson in the title role. It wasn't until 2009 the novel was again adapted, this time with Julia McKenzie as Miss Marple, Joanna Lumley reprising her role as Dolly Bantry and Lindsay Duncan as Marina Gregg. June Whitfield starred as Miss Marple in the August 1998 broadcast of BBC Radio 4's dramatisation. Film director and screenwriter Rituparno Ghosh created a Bengali language version of Christie's story, titled Subho Mahurat (2003), which reset the story in the film industry of Kolkata (Calcutta).",
    "trivia": [],
    "quote": {
      "text": "She had a great power of love and hate but no stability. That's what's so sad for anyone, to be born with no stability.",
      "author": "Miss Marple, The Mirror Crack'd From Side to Side"
    },
    "extractPdf": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/news/Chapter1_The-Mirror-Crackd-from-Side-to-Side_%C2%A9AgathaChristieLimited.pdf",
    "related": [
      {
        "title": "The Thirteen Problems",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-thirteen-problems"
      },
      {
        "title": "A Deadly Wedding Day",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/a-deadly-wedding-day"
      },
      {
        "title": "A Murder is Announced",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/a-murder-is-announced"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "The Clocks",
    "type": "Novel",
    "character": "Hercule Poirot",
    "year": 1963,
    "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-clocks",
    "cover": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/hcus-paperback/Jacket_USTheClocks.jpg",
    "synopsis": "As instructed, stenographer Sheila Webb let herself into the house at 19 Wilbraham Crescent. It was then that she made a grisly discovery: the body of a dead man sprawled across the living room floor.What intrigued Poirot about the case was the time factor. Although in a state of shock, Sheila clearly remembered having heard a cuckoo clock strike three o’clock. Yet, the four other clocks in the living room all showed the time as 4.13. Even more strangely, only one of these clocks belonged to the owner of the house.",
    "moreAbout": "The novel was first serialised in the UK weekly magazine Woman's Own in six installments from 9 November - 14 December 1963 with illustrations by Herb Tauss.\n\nIt was adapted for TV in 2010 in Agatha Christie's Poirot starring David Suchet as Hercule Poirot. The adaptation kept the main plot of the novel but a few changes were made including the location shifting from Dover to Kent. The time period was also changed from the Cold War to before World War II, and a number of characters were either removed or changed.",
    "trivia": [],
    "quote": {
      "text": "One gets infected, it is true, by the style of a work that one has been reading.",
      "author": "Hercule Poirot, The Clocks"
    },
    "extractPdf": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/image-store/Read-Christie-2024/ACL_PoirotList_2024.pdf",
    "related": [
      {
        "title": "The Adventure of the Clapham Cook",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-adventure-of-the-clapham-cook"
      },
