  {
    "title": "Murder On The Nile - Play",
    "type": "Play",
    "character": null,
    "year": 1946,
    "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/murder-on-the-nile",
    "cover": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/hcuk-paperback/Murder-on-the-Nile.jpg",
    "synopsis": "Fatal circumstances await a newlywed couple as they embark on their honeymoon voyage down the Nile.",
    "moreAbout": "Kay Ridgeway has led a charmed life. Blessed with beauty, enormous wealth, and a new husband, she embarks on a honeymoon voyage down the Nile. Fatal circumstances await when the idyllic surroundings are shattered by a shocking and brutal murder. Under scrutiny is a multitude of memorable passengers, all with a reason to kill. The tension and claustrophobia builds, as a shocking and audacious conspiracy is laid bare.\n\nThe play is based on her 1937 novel Death on the Nile. It opened at Dundee Repertory Theatre under the title Hidden Horizon and eventually in at the Ambassadors Theatre in the West End in March 1946.",
    "trivia": [],
    "quote": null,
    "extractPdf": null,
    "related": [
      {
        "title": "Death On The Nile",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/death-on-the-nile"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "The Hollow - Play",
    "type": "Play",
    "character": null,
    "year": 1951,
    "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-hollow-play",
    "cover": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/image-store/058-The-Hollow.png",
    "synopsis": "An unhappy game of romantic follow-the-leader explodes into murder.",
    "moreAbout": "An unhappy game of romantic follow-the-leader explodes into murder one weekend at The Hollow, home of Sir Henry and Lucy Angkatell, arguably Christie’s finest comic grande dame. Dr. Cristow, the Harley Street lothario, is at the center of the trouble when, assembled in one place, we find his dull but devoted wife Gerda, his mistress and prominent sculptor Henrietta and his former lover and Hollywood film star Veronica. Also visiting are Edward and Midge whose romantic assertions are likewise thrown into the mix. As the list of romantic associations grow so does the list of potential suspects when Cristow is shot dead. Nearly everyone has a motive but only one of them did the deed.\n\nThe play was adapted by Agatha Christie from her novel of the same title, The Hollow. The play opened at the Fortune Theatre in the West End in 1951, produced by Peter Saunders. Its success led to Christie giving Saunders the script of The Mousetrap.",
    "trivia": [],
    "quote": {
      "text": "The worst of murder is it does upset the servants so. We were to have duck for lunch. Still, cold duck can be quite nice.",
      "author": "Lady Angkatell"
    },
    "extractPdf": null,
    "related": [
      {
        "title": "The Hollow",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-hollow"
