  {
    "title": "The Mousetrap",
    "type": "Play",
    "character": null,
    "year": 1952,
    "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-mousetrap",
    "cover": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/image-store/Shop-2021/Jacket_Mousetrap70th.jpg",
    "synopsis": "For 70 years, Agatha Christie's The Mousetrap has kept millions of people, from every corner of the globe, on the edge of their seats, and it continues to be a sell-out hit of London theatre. This brand new edition of the world's longest-running play will contain a new introduction by Sophie Hannah, bestselling author of the authorised Agatha Christie Poirot continuation novels, as well as the official play script and a host of exclusive material from the show's archive.\n\nThe scene is set when a group of people gathered in a country house cut off by the snow discover, to their horror, that there is a murderer in their midst. Who can it be? One by one the suspicious characters reveal their sordid pasts until at the last, nerve-shredding moment the identity and the motive are finally revealed.",
    "moreAbout": "This beautiful 70th anniversary edition will contain an introduction by Sophie Hannah, the official playscript, an interview with producer Adam Spiegel, a treasure trove of letters, speeches, photographs, and other fascinating insights into the making of this iconic play. There is also a luxury edition available featuring a beautifully foiled cover, gilded edges, printed endpapers and a red marker ribbon, as well as four A5 postcards illustrating original poster art for The Mousetrap.\n\nAs news spreads of a murder in London, a group of seven strangers find themselves in a remote countryside guesthouse. When a police sergeant arrives, the guests discover - to their horror - that a killer is in their midst. One by one, the suspicious characters reveal their sordid pasts. Which one is the murderer? Who will be their next victim? And can you solve this world-famous mystery for yourself?\n\nThe story was adapted from a radio play, Three Blind Mice, written for the Royal family in 1947. The stage play had to be renamed on the insistence of another producer, Emile Littler, who had used the name on stage before the Second World War, and it was Agatha Christie’s son-in-law, Anthony Hicks who suggested the new title. In fact, it refers to Shakespeare’s Hamlet, in which Hamlet cryptically calls the play depicting the murder of the king, ‘The Mousetrap’.\n\nThe original West End cast included Richard Attenborough and his wife Sheila Sim. One actor has been included in every performance since the opening night and that is Deryck Guyler, whose voice recording reads the radio news bulletin in every show at St Martin’s Theatre.\n\nAgatha Christie gave the rights to The Mousetrap to her grandson Mathew Prichard for his 9th birthday, “Mathew, of course, was always the most lucky member of the family, and it would be Mathew’s gift that turned out the big money winner.” (At the time he wanted a bike.)\n\nIn 1954 the play was one of three Christie's running simultaneously in London’s West End, a feat which made Agatha Christie the first female playwright to achieve.\n\nIn 1997 The Mousetrap Theatre Projects initiative was launched, a charity which helps young people experience London’s theatre, and to which the money from the 25,000th performance was donated.\n\nThe contract terms of the play state that no film version can be made until the West End show has been closed for at least six months (perhaps no one anticipated the show’s success), however an unapproved Russian film version was made in 1990 titled Myshelovka.\n\nBuy tickets to The Mousetrap here.",
    "trivia": [],
    "quote": {
      "text": "Even more thrilling than the plot is the atmosphere of shuddering suspense . . . No one brews it better than Agatha Christie.",
      "author": "Daily Express"
    },
    "extractPdf": null,
    "related": [
      {
        "title": "And Then There Were None",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/and-then-there-were-none"
      },
      {
        "title": "The Hollow",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-hollow"
      },
      {
        "title": "A Murder is Announced",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/a-murder-is-announced"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Witness For The Prosecution - Play",
    "type": "Play",
    "character": null,
    "year": 1953,
    "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/witness-for-the-prosecution-play",
    "cover": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/hcuk-paperback/Witness-for-the-Prosecution.jpg",
    "synopsis": "A thrilling courtroom drama or murder and passion.",
    "moreAbout": "Leonard Vole stands accused of murdering a rich widow. The stakes are high with shocking witness testimony, impassioned outbursts from the dock and a young man’s fight to escape the hangman’s noose. Generally regarded as one of Christie’s most accomplished plays, this suspenseful thriller keeps audiences guessing until the very end.\n\nBy 1954 Witness was one of three Christie plays running simultaneously in the West End, a feat which she was the first female playwright to achieve.",
    "trivia": [],
    "quote": null,
    "extractPdf": null,
    "related": [
      {
        "title": "The Witness for the Prosecution",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/witness-for-the-prosecution"
      },
      {
        "title": "And Then There Were None - Play",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/and-then-there-were-none-play"
      },
      {
        "title": "The Mousetrap",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-mousetrap"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Spider's Web - Play",
    "type": "Play",
    "character": null,
    "year": 1954,
    "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/spiders-web-play",
    "cover": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/hcuk-paperback/Spiders-Web_1.jpg",
    "synopsis": "A comedy thriller in three acts.",
    "moreAbout": "Clarissa, wife of a diplomat, is adept at spinning tales of adventure but when a murder takes place in her drawing room she finds live drama much harder to cope with. Desperate to dispose of the body before her husband arrives with an important politician, she enlists the help of her guests. Hilarity ensues when they are interrupted by the arrival of wry detective, Inspector Lord.\n\nA conscious parody of the detective thriller, Christie delivers a unique blend of suspense and humour. There is tension and laughter in equal parts in an intricate plot of murder, police, drug addicts, invisible ink, hidden doorways and secret drawers.\n\nOriginally written at the request of its star Margaret Lockwood and one of only a few stories to start life as a play, Agatha Christie wrote the play during rehearsals for Witness for the Prosecution, which was then running in London’s West End. The play made its way to the Savoy Theatre in London and ran for 774 performances; joining The Mousetrap and Witness for the Prosecution, making it Christie’s third play to be performed simultaneously in the West End.",
    "trivia": [],
    "quote": null,
    "extractPdf": null,
    "related": [
      {
        "title": "Spider's Web",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/spiders-web"
      },
      {
        "title": "The Hollow - Play",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-hollow-play"
      },
      {
        "title": "The Unexpected Guest - Play",
        "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/the-unexpected-guest-play"
      }
    ]
  },
  {
    "title": "Towards Zero - 1956 Play",
    "type": "Play",
    "character": null,
    "year": 1956,
    "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/towards-zero-play",
    "cover": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/hcuk-paperback/Towards-Zero-v2_1.jpg",
    "synopsis": "A murder mystery set in a clifftop house in three acts.",
    "moreAbout": "When a house party gathers at Gull's Point, the seaside home of Lady Tressilian, Neville Strange finds himself caught between his old wife Audrey and his new flame Kay. A nail-biting thriller, the play probes the psychology of jealousy in the shadow of a savage and brutal murder. A carefully unpeeled investigation before our eyes brings the story to a pointed ending.\n\nThe play, by Agatha Christie and Gerald Verner, premiered in the West End at the St. James's Theatre in September, 1956 produced by Peter Saunders - producer of The Mousetrap.",
    "trivia": [],
    "quote": {
