  {
    "title": "A Christmas Tragedy",
    "type": "Short Story",
    "character": "Miss Marple",
    "year": 1930,
    "url": "https://www.agathachristie.com/stories/a-christmas-tragedy",
    "cover": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/hcus-paperback/Jacket_MidwinterMurderUS.jpg",
    "synopsis": "Miss Marple meets a couple and immediately knows the husband means to kill the wife. But when the wife is found murdered, he has an alibi - could Miss Marple have been wrong? From The Thirteen Problems.",
    "moreAbout": "Miss Marple is certain that Mr Sanders plans to kill his wife, and does everything she can to protect the innocent woman. Despite Miss Marple’s best efforts poor Mrs Sanders is killed but her husband has an alibi – could the amateur sleuth have made a mistake?\n\nMany readers will note the similarities to a plot device used in Agatha Christie's later novel Evil Under the Sun. The story was published with its revised title in the 1932 collection The Thirteen Problems.\n\nIt has never been adapted.",
    "trivia": [],
    "quote": {
      "text": "But facts are facts, and if one is proved to be wrong, one must just be humble about it and start again.",
      "author": "Miss Marple"
    },
    "extractPdf": "https://agathachristie.imgix.net/image-store/Read-Christie-2024/ACL_MarpleList_2024.pdf",
